prime minister justin trudeau is on his  way to rwanda for a summit with  commonwealth leaders the first such  meeting since 2018. from there trudeau  will travel to germany to meet with g7  leaders before attending the nato summit  in spain russia's war in ukraine  expected to dominate all three meetings  our chief political correspondent david  aiken is traveling with the prime  minister david  well they do prime minister justin  trudeau is going to once again call on  world leaders at all these summits to do  whatever they can to get russia to  withdraw from ukraine but world leaders  including the prime minister are also  seized with one of the big problems  caused by russia's illegal invasion of  ukraine global food insecurity mps from  all parties on the house of commons  agriculture committee understand the  urgency of the issue and are already  looking at ways canada might help i  think it's one of the most important  questions that we all collectively in  the world are facing ukraine and russia  are two of the world's largest food  producers  war has not only disrupted that  production but russia is now actively  targeting ukraine's agricultural  capacity and has destroyed some stores  of grain at black sea ports and given  the fact that ukraine  feeds about 400 million people every  year  we know that uh  that the world is is in trouble  we're gonna run out of food but leaders  at this trio of summits the commonwealth  the g7 and nato could struggle to meet  that challenge the world is rather  volatile  and  economic conditions  may make things uh difficult  because if you're looking at a recession  there won't be quite as much money to  hand around this is not just a 2022  problem the consequences of the war are  going to have reverberating impacts far  beyond 2022 and trudeau may face  difficult questions about what canada is  doing to help it is high time for for  canadian farming or canaan  ag to really think differently about  how to support the entire world when it  comes to food security because i don't  think canada's role has been all that  significant trudeau will touch down in  the rwandan capital of kigali wednesday  afternoon and he won't be back here in  ottawa until canada day nitu thanks  david
 

